# Always Add Tests for Fixed Issues

**Hard rule for tektonize:** every bugfix, diagnostic false-positive fix,
emit/compile fix, or other conversion defect must ship with a **regression test
in the same change**. No exceptions for “small” or “obvious” fixes.

A change that only patches production code without a test that would fail if the
bug returned is incomplete — add the test before finishing.

## Required

- Cover the **exact failure mode** that was fixed (minimal Groovy/Python snippet
  or fixture is enough — prefer small focused cases over huge library dumps).
- Prefer `tests/test_regression_parse.py` for parse/CST/report regressions; use
  the nearest existing module (`test_transformers.py`, `test_e2e.py`, …) when
  that is clearly a better fit.
- Name or docstring the test so it records *what used to break* (e.g. empty map
  `[:]`, optional `;`, nested ERROR noise).
- Assert observable behavior (diagnostics, emitted Python, `py_compile`, stage
  `conversion_report.yaml` shape) rather than private implementation details.
- Run `make test` (or targeted pytest) and confirm the new test passes with the
  fix and would catch a revert of the fix.

## Do not

- Ship a fix with only manual verification and no automated coverage
- Delete or weaken a regression test to make an unrelated change pass
- Rely solely on converting a private full shared library as the only check

